          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have had a cough for two weeks after a cold.",
          "answer": "Your cough for two weeks after a cold requires routine care. This means it's not urgent, but if your symptoms worsen, or if you develop new or unexplained symptoms, it would be wise to seek medical advice.\n\nThis cough is mainly associated with the respiratory system. After a cold, it is common for a cough to persist for up to three weeks. It's often part of the normal recovery process and typically isn't a sign of a bacterial infection or treatment failure. According to the **Upper respiratory infection (common cold)** document, coughs following colds might last longer than other symptoms. \n\nOther potential causes of a lingering cough could include asthma or gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD), both of which can cause chronic coughs under certain conditions. Flu may also present with a cough that lasts for a similar timeframe, especially when it doesn't resolve quickly.\n\nFor a thorough evaluation of your symptoms, discussing them with a clinician may help clarify the underlying cause and any necessary management.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
