          "turn_index": 1,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Would antibiotics help?",
          "answer": "Antibiotics are effective against bacterial infections but not against viral infections. Therefore, they are not appropriate for conditions like the common cold or influenza, as these are caused by viruses. \n\nAccording to the **Guideline on upper respiratory infection**, antibiotics should only be considered if there are clear signs of a bacterial infection, such as:\n\n1. Symptoms that are severe or not improving after several days.\n2. The presence of specific complications, like bacterial sinusitis or pneumonia.\n3. A high fever or other significant symptoms that suggest a bacterial cause.\n\nIn most cases of a cough following a cold, antibiotics are not warranted because the cough is likely due to the residual effects of the viral infection. It is essential to discuss with a clinician whether your symptoms may indicate a bacterial infection that would require antibiotics.\n\nAgain, antibiotics should not be used indiscriminately, as their misuse can contribute to antibiotic resistance and other adverse effects. \n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.\n[sources: guideline-uri-antibiotic-stewardship]",
